<span class="ltx_personname">Nachiketa Patil
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Centre for Machine Intelligence and Data Science, IIT Bombay
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">npatil@minds.iitb.ac.in</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Suraj Racha
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Koita Centre for Digital Health, IIT Bombay
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">23d1627@iitb.ac.in</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ganesh Ramakrishnan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science and Engineering, IIT Bombay
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ganesh@cse.iitb.ac.in</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct Preference Optimisation (DPO)</span> has emerged as a powerful method for aligning Large Language Models (LLMs) with human preferences, offering a stable and efficient alternative to approaches that use Reinforcement learning via Human Feedback. In this work, we investigate the performance of DPO using open-source preference datasets. One of the major drawbacks of DPO is that it doesn’t induce granular scoring and treats all the segments of the responses with equal propensity. However, this is not practically true for human preferences since even “good” responses have segments that may not be preferred by the annotator. To resolve this, a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2-dimensional</span> scoring for DPO alignment called <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2D-DPO</span> was proposed. We explore the 2D-DPO alignment paradigm and the advantages it provides over the standard DPO by comparing their win rates. It is observed that these methods, even though effective, are not robust to label/score noise. To counter this, we propose an approach of incorporating <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">segment-level</span> score noise robustness to the 2D-DPO algorithm. Along with theoretical backing, we also provide empirical verification in favour of the algorithm and introduce other noise models that can be present.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) and their increase in popularity, discussions and debates about the safety and correctness of model responses have become crucial. Having an LLM that gives the correct answer is not sufficient anymore - we need models that give “safe” and “responsible” answers. This idea of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">aligning</span> LLMs to human preferences has taken priority in the community ( Liu et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, Shen et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, Wang et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>) and multiple methods have been proposed for the same. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since most LLM responses need to be aligned to human preferences, the intuitive way to perform alignment is to include <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Feedback</span> in the training process. Often termed as <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reinforcement Learning with Human Feedback (RLHF)</span> (as proposed by Christiano et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>), the algorithm performs alignment usign <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pairwise preferences</span> provided by the human annotators between the responses. The algorithm follows a two-step process, which first learns a reward model to maximise reward scores between the two responses and then models an LLM policy around that reward model. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although effective, this method is computationally heavy and unstable due to the two-step optimization process. A more elegant solution was proposed by Rafailov et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> in the form of <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct Preference Optimisation (DPO)</span>. DPO performs a single-step optimisation where the LLM policy is modelled directly using the annotated preference dataset. This simplified the overall process, was computationally lighter, while being on par with RLHF when it came to performance. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Over the years, many extensions to DPO have been proposed, each optimising some aspect of the original algorithm. In their recent work, Li et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> claimed that because DPO assigns a single score to preferred and rejected responses, it fails to capture the granularity of human responses. To correct for this drawback, they proposed <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2-dimensional DPO (2D-DPO)</span> that divides the response into <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">segments</span> and each segment is scored across <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">5 aspects</span> - “Completeness”, “Clarity”, “Correctness”, “Safety” and “Helpfulness”. Backing their claim with empirical evidence, they showed that this paradigm improved upon the performance of Vanilla DPO. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The algorithms discussed thus far are effective for preference alignment, but work under the assumption that the reward scores provided by the human annotators are the ground truth. In practical settings however, there is a significant chance that some of these labels and scores are <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">noisy</span> and do not reflect the true preference of the user. Robustness to such noisy labels in vanilla DPO was explored by Chowdhury et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> where they handled the probabilistic flipping of labels between rejected and preferred responses. However, we were unable to find a similar paradigm for 2D-DPO. In this work, we propose a <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Robust 2D-DPO</span> framework that is robust to perturbations at the segment-level scores. We provide theoretical formulations and empirical verifications for a framework that is robust to small, segment-level perturbations drawn uniformly from a distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback.</span> Emperical evidence has suggested the efficacy of incorporating human feedback and preferences in domains like robotics (Abramson et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>) and LLM training (Ziegler et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>) to mention a few. In the case of LLM training, <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-inforcement Learning using Human Feedback (RLHF)</span> as proposed by Christiano et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> has been a pivotal algorithm for preference optimisation. Using a two-step process of reward model formulation and policy modelling, RLHF has proven effective in optimising pairwise human preferences in LLM responses.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct Preference Optimisation.</span> Although RLHF has proven effective in homogeneous and heterogeneous preference settings (Park. et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>), it is complex and faces instability issues (Casper et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>) during training. To overcome this, Rafailov et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> proposed Direct Preference Optimisation that performed preference optimisation a single step instead. DPO provided several computational and stability advantages over RLHF (Liu et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>) that ensured its popularity in the community. As a result, multiple DPO variants have emerged (Zeng et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, Lanchantin et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, Shao et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, Zhou et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>) that improve upon certain aspects of the original DPO algorithm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2-Dimensional DPO.</span> Prefernce optimisation algorithms as a whole operate by assigning a single score to the entire LLM response that shall distinguish the preferred and rejected responses. This assumption inhibits the algorithms to capture true “granularity” of human preferences. Human preference inherently have multiple <span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">aspects</span> (Stokes et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>) that are not captured by regular preference optimisation models. Li et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> proposed the 2-dimension DPO (2D-DPO) which would score each segment of the response across 5 segments - <span id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Completeness, Clarity, Correctness, Safety</span> &amp; <span id="S2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Helpfulness</span>. A combination of these scores then create a more holistic view of human preferences and enable more accuracy preference optimisation in LLMs. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robust DPO.</span> Preference optimisation algorithms also operate under another major assumption which is that human annotations are the ground truth. Practically, this assumption doesn’t always hold true as there can be noise in human labels and scores (Wei et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>). Specifically, noisy (incorrect and ambiguous) preference pairs in the dataset might restrict the language models from capturing human intent accurately. While practitioners have recently proposed heuristics to mitigate the effect of noisy preferences, a complete theoretical understanding of their workings remained elusive till Chowdhury et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> proposed Robust DPO - a general framework for policy optimisation in the presence of random preference flips. This was achieved by using a novel loss function, which de-biases the effect of noise on average, making a policy trained by minimizing a loss robust to the noise. Although this was performed for Vanilla DPO, there is a need to design a similar framework for other DPO techniques. Our work aims to add a sense of robustness similar to the work by Chowdhury et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> in a 2D-DPO setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Problem Setup and Background</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Preference optimisation algorithms take in preference dataset <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{\left\{{s_{i}},{a_{w}^{i}},{a_{l}^{i}}\right\}}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{\left\{{s_{i}},{a_{w}^{i}},{a_{l}^{i}}\right\}}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{s_{i}}\sim\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{s_{i}}\sim\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is a prompt sampled from the space of possible prompts and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a_{w}^{i}},{a_{l}^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a_{w}^{i}},{a_{l}^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the preferred and rejected responses respectively. Assuming a BTL model (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>) and a latent reward model <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r^{\ast}}\hskip 2.84544pt\colon\hskip 2.84544pt\left(s,a\right)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r^{\ast}}\hskip 2.84544pt\colon\hskip 2.84544pt\left(s,a\right)\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, RLHF (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>) aims to perform the following 2 steps -</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It learns an optimal reward model <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the margin between the scores for <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is maximized across prompts and responses.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Then, it uses <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> to find an optimal policy <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the probability of getting <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{w}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{w}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for prompt <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> across all <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation for RLHF results in the Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 Problem Setup and Background ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> which gives the optimisation problem for getting the optimal policy -</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\pi^{\ast}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\arg\max_{\pi}\hskip 2.84544ptJ(\pi)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\arg\max_{\pi}\hskip 2.84544pt\mathbb{E}_{s\sim\rho,\hskip 1.42271pta\sim\pi(.\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pts)}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{r^{\ast}}(s,a)\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\beta\log\left(\frac{\pi(a|s)}{{\pi^{\text{SFT}}}(a|s)}\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo rspace="0.307em">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.307em">.</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.307em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pi^{\ast}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\arg\max_{\pi}\hskip 2.84544ptJ(\pi)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\arg\max_{\pi}\hskip 2.84544pt\mathbb{E}_{s\sim\rho,\hskip 1.42271pta\sim\pi(.\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pts)}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{r^{\ast}}(s,a)\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\beta\log\left(\frac{\pi(a|s)}{{\pi^{\text{SFT}}}(a|s)}\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This follows from the usage of the BTL model (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>) where the probability of choosing the preferred response over the rejected response for a prompt <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is given in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 Problem Setup and Background ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\left({a_{w}}\succ{a_{l}}\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\sigma\left[{r^{\ast}}(s,{a_{w}})\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{r^{\ast}}(s,{a_{l}})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\left({a_{w}}\succ{a_{l}}\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\sigma\left[{r^{\ast}}(s,{a_{w}})\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{r^{\ast}}(s,{a_{l}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Rafailov et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> proposed <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct Preference Optimisation</span> that would write the reward model in terms of the policy and directly optimize over the policy in a single step. This formulation was more stable and resulted in the optimal policy as mentioned in Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 Problem Setup and Background ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}{\pi^{\ast}}&amp;\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\arg\max_{\theta}\hskip 2.84544pt\mathcal{L}\left(\theta\hskip 1.42271pt;\hskip 1.42271pts,{a_{w}},{a_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt\\
&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-\log\sigma\left[\beta\log\left(\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}({a_{w}}|s)}{{\pi^{\text{SFT}}}({a_{w}}|s)}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\beta\log\left(\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}({a_{l}}|s)}{{\pi^{\text{SFT}}}({a_{l}}|s)}\right)\right]\\
&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-\log\sigma\left(\beta{h_{\theta}}\left(s,{a_{w}},{a_{l}}\right)\right)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.838em">=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.140em" rspace="0.307em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.560em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.782em">−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}{\pi^{\ast}}&amp;\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\arg\max_{\theta}\hskip 2.84544pt\mathcal{L}\left(\theta\hskip 1.42271pt;\hskip 1.42271pts,{a_{w}},{a_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt\\
&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-\log\sigma\left[\beta\log\left(\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}({a_{w}}|s)}{{\pi^{\text{SFT}}}({a_{w}}|s)}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\beta\log\left(\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}({a_{l}}|s)}{{\pi^{\text{SFT}}}({a_{l}}|s)}\right)\right]\\
&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-\log\sigma\left(\beta{h_{\theta}}\left(s,{a_{w}},{a_{l}}\right)\right)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{h_{\theta}}\left(s,{a_{w}},{a_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{\theta}}(s,{a_{w}})-{r_{\theta}}(s,{a_{l}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{h_{\theta}}\left(s,{a_{w}},{a_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{\theta}}(s,{a_{w}})-{r_{\theta}}(s,{a_{l}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The mathematical proof for the above expression is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof for Direct Preference Optimisation ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Eq. <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 Problem Setup and Background ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is the vanilla DPO loss formulation. However, this formulation is not robust to label noise in the dataset. Chowdhury et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> proposed a more robust formulation of DPO in which noise was induced in the preferences via the standard random noise model, where the revealed preferences are true preferences flipped with a small probability <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in\left(0,\frac{1}{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in\left(0,\frac{1}{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> i.e.</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}_{\epsilon}}\left[\left(\tilde{a_{w}},\tilde{a_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\left({a_{l}},{a_{w}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right]\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\epsilon" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}_{\epsilon}}\left[\left(\tilde{a_{w}},\tilde{a_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\left({a_{l}},{a_{w}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right]\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to Vanilla DPO, the loss function for the noisy dataset can be written as seen in Eq. <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3 Problem Setup and Background ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L_{\epsilon}}\left(\theta\hskip 1.42271pt;\hskip 1.42271pts,\tilde{a_{w}},\tilde{a_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt-\log{\mathbb{P}_{\theta,\epsilon}}\left[\tilde{a_{w}}\succ\tilde{a_{l}}\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.140em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≻</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L_{\epsilon}}\left(\theta\hskip 1.42271pt;\hskip 1.42271pts,\tilde{a_{w}},\tilde{a_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt-\log{\mathbb{P}_{\theta,\epsilon}}\left[\tilde{a_{w}}\succ\tilde{a_{l}}\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">However, this formulation has a major drawback as it introduces a <span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bias</span> in the DPO loss. This is because -</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{logit}\left({\mathbb{P}_{\theta}}\left[{a_{w}}\succ{a_{l}}\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right]\right)\hskip 2.84544pt\neq\hskip 2.84544pt\mathrm{logit}\left({\mathbb{P}_{\theta,\epsilon}}\left[{a_{w}}\succ{a_{l}}\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right]\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>logit</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≠</mo><mrow><mi>logit</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{logit}\left({\mathbb{P}_{\theta}}\left[{a_{w}}\succ{a_{l}}\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right]\right)\hskip 2.84544pt\neq\hskip 2.84544pt\mathrm{logit}\left({\mathbb{P}_{\theta,\epsilon}}\left[{a_{w}}\succ{a_{l}}\hskip 2.84544pt|\hskip 2.84544pts\right]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, there is a need to modify the formulation to take care of the bias. An unbiased loss formulation was presented by Chowdhury et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> as shown in <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3 Problem Setup and Background ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\epsilon}}\left(\theta\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pts,{\tilde{a}_{w}},{\tilde{a}_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\frac{\left(1-\epsilon\right)\mathcal{L}\left(\theta\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pts,{\tilde{a}_{w}},{\tilde{a}_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\epsilon\mathcal{L}\left(\theta\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pts,{\tilde{a}_{l}},{\tilde{a}_{2}}\right)}{1-2\epsilon}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.280em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.280em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.280em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\epsilon}}\left(\theta\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pts,{\tilde{a}_{w}},{\tilde{a}_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\frac{\left(1-\epsilon\right)\mathcal{L}\left(\theta\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pts,{\tilde{a}_{w}},{\tilde{a}_{l}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\epsilon\mathcal{L}\left(\theta\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pts,{\tilde{a}_{l}},{\tilde{a}_{2}}\right)}{1-2\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">A detailed derivation and mathematical proof as to why this loss is unbiased is presented in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Robust DPO formulation ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous section, we explored a robust model to take care of noisy labels in Vanilla DPO (when <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is small) as proposed by Chowdhury et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Although robust, this method still doesn’t enjoy the granularity that 2D-DPO offers and hence, a clear need to extend robustness to the 2D-DPO paradigm has been raised. The algorithm proposed by us in this work aims to induce robustness in the 2D-DPO algorithm (as proposed by Li et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>) at a <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">segment level</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Understanding 2D-DPO</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to induce robustness in 2D-DPO, we first need to explore the Vanilla 2D-DPO algorithm as proposed by Li et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. 2D-DPO takes a prompt as an input and each prompt has a preferred response <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left({\tau_{w}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left({\tau_{w}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and a rejected response <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left({\tau_{l}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left({\tau_{l}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. These responses are further divided into segments by being split over grammatical sentence separators (like periods, commas, etc.). Consider (without a loss of generality) that the winner (preferred) response <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> number of segments after splitting and similarly, the loser (rejected) response <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> segments. As <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be equal, we take inspiration from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and define</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\min\left({N_{w}},{N_{l}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\min\left({N_{w}},{N_{l}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then, we proceed to choose the top-<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> segments of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> while choosing the bottom-<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> segments of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Choosing the top-<math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> segments from the winner response and bottom-<math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> segments from the loser response is done to maximize the margin between the two.</span></span></span>
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The segments of the responses are indexed by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{0,1,\ldots,N-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{0,1,\ldots,N-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and for the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\mathrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>th</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\mathrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> segment, token-level DPO (as proposed by Zeng et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>) is performed to calculate the probability of getting token <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> given token <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has arrived. Easch of the segments is further scored based on <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5 aspects</span> - ‘‘Completeness’’, ‘‘Clarity’’, ‘‘Correctness’’, ‘‘Safety’’ and ‘‘Helpfulness’’. These scores are assigned to each segment by external annotators <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            In the work proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, GPT-4 Turbo is used to simulate external annotators and provide the aspect scores. Check Appendix F of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for more details</span></span></span>. Each segment is given an integer score between 0 and 4 (both inclusive) across an aspect where score 4 reflects high desirability and score 0 reflects undesirability.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These aspect scores are then combined in a weighted combination to get the score of the segment as shown in Eq. <a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}{r_{w,k}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{w^{T}}{r_{k}}\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{completeness}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{completeness}}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt+\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{clarity}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{clarity}}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt+\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{correctness}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{correctness}}}\right)\\
&amp;+\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{safety}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{safety}}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt+\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{helpfulness}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{helpfulness}}}\right)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.838em" rspace="0.838em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.838em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>completeness</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.362em" rspace="0.502em">∗</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>completeness</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.560em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.782em">+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>clarity</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.362em" rspace="0.502em">∗</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>clarity</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.560em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.782em">+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>correctness</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.362em" rspace="0.502em">∗</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>correctness</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.280em">+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>safety</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.362em" rspace="0.502em">∗</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>safety</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.560em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.782em">+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>helpfulness</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.362em" rspace="0.502em">∗</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>helpfulness</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}{r_{w,k}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{w^{T}}{r_{k}}\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{completeness}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{completeness}}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt+\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{clarity}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{clarity}}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt+\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{correctness}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{correctness}}}\right)\\
&amp;+\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{safety}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{safety}}}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt+\hskip 2.84544pt\left({w_{\mathrm{helpfulness}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\ast\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{\mathrm{helpfulness}}}\right)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In Eq. <a href="#S4.E8" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r_{w,k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r_{w,k}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the score for the <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\mathrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>th</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\mathrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> segment of the winner response <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and this is a weighted combination of the scores this segment has gotten across the aspects. One thing to note here is that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sum_{i=1}^{5}}\hskip 2.84544pt{w_{i}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt1\hskip 14.22636pt;\hskip 14.22636ptw_{i}\geq 0\hskip 5.69046pt\forall\hskip 2.84544pti" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1.42em;" width="1.42em"></mspace></mrow><mo rspace="1.587em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.737em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sum_{i=1}^{5}}\hskip 2.84544pt{w_{i}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt1\hskip 14.22636pt;\hskip 14.22636ptw_{i}\geq 0\hskip 5.69046pt\forall\hskip 2.84544pti</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the segment score is a convex combination of the aspect scores for that segment and thus <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r_{w,k}}\in[0,4]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r_{w,k}}\in[0,4]</annotation></semantics></math> i.e. the segment scores are real numbers between 0 and 4 (both inclusive). This definition of the segment and aspect scores can now be used to formulate the loss function for the 2D-DPO paradigm as illustrated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\{\beta\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{w,k}}{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}\\
&amp;-\hskip 2.84544pt\beta\hskip 1.42271pt{{r}_{l,k}}{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}\}]\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>group</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.338em">=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.307em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.307em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.782em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.307em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\{\beta\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{w,k}}{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}\\
&amp;-\hskip 2.84544pt\beta\hskip 1.42271pt{{r}_{l,k}}{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}\}]\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> with <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 Problem Setup and Background ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we can see that there is a similarity with the original DPO paradigm. The intuition to maximize the difference between the token log-probalities of the winner and loser responses still holds in Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. However, now each of the log-probability values is being calculated for a segment and then weighted with the segment scores. Then, these differences are fed to the log-sigmoid function to get the preference log-probabilities as per the <span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BTL - model</span> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>). This would give us the log-probabilities of the <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\mathrm{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>th</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\mathrm{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> segments of the preferred and rejected responses. The differences are then summed over all segments and the expectation over the responses for this quantity completes the loss function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>High-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the loss formulation in Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> being more fine-grained when compared to the Vanilla DPO loss function, it is not robust to noise in the scores. Since the scores for each segment across aspects are given by external annotators, these scores may have some level of label noise, and a robust formulation is needed to handle this noise. As per the formulation in Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, there can be multiple places where noise could be induced in the model. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The most high-level noise could take the form of <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">“preference flips”</span> i.e. the annotator labels the preferred response as the rejected response and vice-versa with some probability <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Mathematically, this would mean</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r_{w,k}}{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}\hskip 8.5359pt\Longleftrightarrow\hskip 8.5359pt{{r}_{l,k}}{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}\hskip 14.22636pt\text{with probability $\gamma$}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="1.128em" rspace="1.128em" stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.42em;" width="1.42em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>with probability </mtext><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r_{w,k}}{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}\hskip 8.5359pt\Longleftrightarrow\hskip 8.5359pt{{r}_{l,k}}{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}\hskip 14.22636pt\text{with probability $\gamma$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In simpler terms, the sign of the quantity inside the sigmoid in Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> will be flipped. If the sum of the token-level log-probabilities weighted by the segment rewards evaluate to <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0</span>, then Vanilla 2D-DPO shall remain robust to this noise model. This follows from Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ 4.2 High-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> begin defined as the standard Sigmoid function, we have -</span></p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\left[\sigma(x)\right]=\log\left[\sigma(-x)\right]\hskip 8.5359pt\Longleftrightarrow\hskip 8.5359ptx=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.850em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.128em" stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\left[\sigma(x)\right]=\log\left[\sigma(-x)\right]\hskip 8.5359pt\Longleftrightarrow\hskip 8.5359ptx=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ 4.2 High-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> has been deferred to Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Proof of Lemma ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If one notices carefully, then this noise model is almost the same one that Chowdhury et. al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> consider in their work and thus, the unbiased loss estimator in Eq. <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3 Problem Setup and Background ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> works for our use case as well. However, the loss <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\left(\theta\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pts,{\tilde{a}_{w}},{\tilde{a}_{l}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.280em">,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\left(\theta\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pts,{\tilde{a}_{w}},{\tilde{a}_{l}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> will now be taken in from the 2D-DPO loss formulation in Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. The noisy-robust unbiased loss estimator in this case can be given as shown in Eq. <a href="#S4.E11" title="In 4.2 High-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\gamma}}\left(\pi_{\theta}\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pt\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\frac{\left(1-\gamma\right){\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\gamma{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)}{1-2\gamma}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>group</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>group</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\gamma}}\left(\pi_{\theta}\hskip 2.84544pt;\hskip 2.84544pt\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\frac{\left(1-\gamma\right){\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\gamma{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)}{1-2\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Segment-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The loss mentioned in the previous section is a high-level noise where entire response preferences are flipped with some probability. However, this flipping is not practically observed, unlike <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">segment-level</span> noise. In this case, there is a small perturbation <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> added to the segment score <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{w,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{w,k}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{l,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{l,k}</annotation></semantics></math>. This perturbation is uniformly sampled from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> since having a perturbation beyond 1 can induce a large amount of error that might not be manageable. Let us consider the loss function of 2D-DPO as written in Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> -</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>group</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\{\beta{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{w,k}}\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.058em">=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>β</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\{\beta{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt{r_{w,k}}\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle-\hskip 2.84544pt\beta{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt{{r}_{l,k}}\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}\}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\hskip 2.84544pt\beta{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt{{r}_{l,k}}\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}\}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify the notation, let us define the following terms.</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\beta{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}\hskip 14.22636pt;\hskip 14.22636pt{l_{(l,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\beta{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.42em;" width="1.42em"></mspace></mrow><mo rspace="1.587em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.140em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\beta{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{w}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{w}^{t}}\right)}\hskip 14.22636pt;\hskip 14.22636pt{l_{(l,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt\beta{\sum_{t={n_{k}}}^{{n_{k}}+{l_{k}}}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\frac{{\pi_{\theta}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}{{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}}\left({a_{l}^{t}}\hskip 1.42271pt|\hskip 1.42271pt{s_{l}^{t}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting from Eq. <a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.3 Segment-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> back into Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.1 Understanding 2D-DPO ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we get -</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{r_{(w,k)}}{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(l,k)}}{l_{(l,k)}}\right\}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>group</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{group}}}\left({\pi_{\theta}};\mathcal{D}\right)\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{r_{(w,k)}}{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(l,k)}}{l_{(l,k)}}\right\}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r_{(w,k)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r_{(w,k)}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r_{(l,k)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r_{(l,k)}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the segment scores for a segment <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of the preferred and rejected responses respectively. To these scores, let us now add a small perturbation <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> to get the noisy scores as seen in Eq. <a href="#S4.E14" title="In 4.3 Segment-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{r}_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(w,k)}}-\delta\hskip 14.22636pt;\hskip 14.22636pt{\hat{r}_{(l,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(l,k)}}+\delta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.42em;" width="1.42em"></mspace></mrow><mo rspace="1.587em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{r}_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(w,k)}}-\delta\hskip 14.22636pt;\hskip 14.22636pt{\hat{r}_{(l,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(l,k)}}+\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The perturbation shall lower the preferred response’s segment scores while it shall increase the loser response’s segment scores. This noise model is aimed to reduce the margin between the 2 scores and hence induce a level of confusion in the model. Substituting the noisy rewards from Eq. <a href="#S4.E14" title="In 4.3 Segment-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> back into the loss function as defined in Eq. <a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4.3 Segment-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, we get the noisy loss function -</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\hat{L}({\pi_{\theta}},\mathcal{D},\delta)\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{\hat{r}_{(w,k)}}{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{\hat{r}_{(l,k)}}{l_{(l,k)}}\right\}\right]\\
\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{\left({r_{(w,k)}}-\delta\right){l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\left({r_{(l,k)}}+\delta\right){l_{(l,k)}}\right\}\right]\\
\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{r_{(w,k)}}{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(l,k)}}{l_{(l,k)}}-\delta\left({l_{(w,k)}}+{l_{(l,k)}}\right)\right\}\right]\\
\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{X_{k}}-\delta{Y_{k}}\right\}\right]\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.307em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.307em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.782em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.307em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.782em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.307em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\hat{L}({\pi_{\theta}},\mathcal{D},\delta)\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{\hat{r}_{(w,k)}}{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{\hat{r}_{(l,k)}}{l_{(l,k)}}\right\}\right]\\
\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{\left({r_{(w,k)}}-\delta\right){l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt\left({r_{(l,k)}}+\delta\right){l_{(l,k)}}\right\}\right]\\
\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{r_{(w,k)}}{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(l,k)}}{l_{(l,k)}}-\delta\left({l_{(w,k)}}+{l_{(l,k)}}\right)\right\}\right]\\
\hskip 2.84544pt&amp;=\hskip 2.84544pt-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{X_{k}}-\delta{Y_{k}}\right\}\right]\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Where</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{X_{k}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(w,k)}}{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(l,k)}}{l_{(l,k)}}\hskip 14.22636pt;\hskip 14.22636pt{Y_{k}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{l_{(w,k)}}+{l_{(l,k)}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.42em;" width="1.42em"></mspace></mrow><mo rspace="1.587em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{X_{k}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(w,k)}}{l_{(w,k)}}\hskip 2.84544pt-\hskip 2.84544pt{r_{(l,k)}}{l_{(l,k)}}\hskip 14.22636pt;\hskip 14.22636pt{Y_{k}}\hskip 2.84544pt=\hskip 2.84544pt{l_{(w,k)}}+{l_{(l,k)}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, our optimisation problem has now become -</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\text{minimize}\hskip 14.22636pt&amp;-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{X_{k}}-\delta{Y_{k}}\right\}\right]\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mtext>minimize</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.307em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\text{minimize}\hskip 14.22636pt&amp;-{\mathbb{E}_{\delta\sim U(0,1)}}{\mathbb{E}_{\left({\tau_{w}},{\tau_{l}}\right)\sim\mathcal{D}}}\hskip 2.84544pt\left[{\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}}\hskip 1.42271pt\log\sigma\left\{{X_{k}}-\delta{Y_{k}}\right\}\right]\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The optimisation problem in Eq. <a href="#S4.E17" title="In 4.3 Segment-Level Noise-Robust 2D-DPO Formulation ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> is unconstrained in <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> while being constrained between <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and doesn’t have a closed form solution. Such loss functions are solved using <span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient-based</span> methods. We will be using Stochastic Gradient Descent in this work, as shown in the algorithm in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Noisy 2D-DPO Algorithm ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>
<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
        <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
        
        
        
      Code and Experiment details (to replicate) can be found <a href="https://github.com/nachiketapatil/Robust-2D-DPO" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">here</a></span></span></span>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We performed experiments to first create a base line and then compare our algorithm against said baseline. Tab <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Inducing Robustness in a 2-dimensional Direct Preference Optimisation Paradigm" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> contains the list of experiments performed in this work. The model used in all the experiments is <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pythia 6.9B</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
